// Enables Gobra in the current file.
// +gobra

// This file holds the trusted members of package bytealg: specifications that
// Gobra takes on faith rather than proving. Everything here is part of the
// trusted computing base and deserves scrutiny in review.
//
//   - Equal          implemented in per-architecture assembly (equal_*.s),
//                    which Gobra cannot verify.
//   - lemmaBitFacts  two facts about Go's >> and & on non-negative operands
//                    that the PowRK loops rely on.

package bytealg

// Equal reports whether a and b are the same length and contain the same
// bytes. A nil argument is equivalent to an empty slice.
//
// Equal is declared in equal_native.go and implemented in per-architecture
// assembly files (equal_*.s), which Gobra cannot verify. This bodyless pure
// function is its trusted specification: two byte slices are Equal exactly
// when they have the same (mathematical) sequence of bytes.
trusted
requires acc(a) && acc(b)
ensures res == (seq(a) == seq(b))
decreases
pure func Equal(a, b []byte) (res bool)

// lemmaBitFacts states the two facts about Go's >> and & operators on
// non-negative operands that the verification of the PowRK loops relies on.
//
// ASSUMPTION: this lemma is trusted. Gobra encodes the bitwise operators as
// uninterpreted functions without any axioms, so these (true) facts of Go's
// semantics cannot be proven inside Gobra. They are standard: for i >= 0,
// shifting right by one is division by two, and masking with 1 yields the
// remainder modulo two.
ghost
trusted
requires 0 <= i
ensures i>>1 == i/2
ensures i&1 == i%2
decreases
func lemmaBitFacts(i int)
